WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.919 --> 00:00:17.919
[Music]

00:00:17.919 --> 00:00:21.530
Mr. Loescher, thank you very much for being with us here

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:23.750
today. If we can start off as Dean

00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:26.099
[inaudible] said, you have worked in many different

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:28.449
countries across the world and worked in many different

00:00:28.469 --> 00:00:30.949
businesses. Perhaps we could start by asking you, there are

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:33.749
some common traits that you have identified with all of the

00:00:33.769 --> 00:00:36.269
leaders that you have met that you would say define

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:38.449
leadership for you.

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:40.619
I think there are many, many definitions of leaderships

00:00:40.619 --> 00:00:44.379
around the world so I will not be the one who will add

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:49.549
another one. But I mean if you think about maybe we can talk

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:54.569
a little bit in terms of what I personally consider as key

00:00:54.589 --> 00:01:00.009
traits of good leaders. I think the first one I would say is

00:01:00.029 --> 00:01:08.109
clarity of direction and vision. Second one is, and that's

00:01:08.129 --> 00:01:11.620
probably a skill in itself that how you break it then down

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.739
in actionable steps shorter, medium and long term which is

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:20.459
then linked to the capacity of the organization and to your

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:23.400
competitive environment which you are facing so that you

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.829
challenge the organization, that you challenge the

00:01:25.849 --> 00:01:30.049
environment but you make the possible, possible in the

00:01:30.069 --> 00:01:35.489
fastest time what you can and what you are able to implement

00:01:35.509 --> 00:01:39.829
so how you execute well in this regard. The third one I

00:01:39.849 --> 00:01:45.379
would say is that you have to be absolutely passionate

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:47.719
because without passion you will not be able to rally your

00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:52.799
teams behind you. The fourth one I would say you have to be

00:01:52.819 --> 00:01:57.980
absolutely determined in pursuing what you have set out for

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.099
the organization and for me, the fifth one I would say is

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:08.749
that you have to have a true north for yourself irrespective

00:02:08.769 --> 00:02:15.059
of company values or company culture that you clearly define

00:02:15.079 --> 00:02:21.769
for yourself what's right and what's wrong and then you are

00:02:21.789 --> 00:02:23.369
on to the field to play.

00:02:23.389 --> 00:02:27.669
And on to the field you were as Dean sort of said the first

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:31.669
outside CEO in Siemens's history. I imagine that created

00:02:31.689 --> 00:02:34.249
some specific leadership challenges for you, perhaps you

00:02:34.269 --> 00:02:36.929
could talk about these principles that you mentioned and how

00:02:36.949 --> 00:02:41.089
you applied those as you took over as CEO and President of

00:02:41.109 --> 00:02:42.959
Siemens.

00:02:42.979 --> 00:02:46.719
I mean who has actually read and worked through the case,

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:49.989
can you just show me by hands up, well I don't have to

00:02:49.989 --> 00:02:55.389
explain anything o you. I mean obviously it was a very

00:02:55.409 --> 00:02:58.019
defining moment for the company, it was very defining moment

00:02:58.039 --> 00:03:01.310
for myself. I mean I was the 12th CEO in the history of the

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.239
company, the first one coming from the outside, and the

00:03:05.259 --> 00:03:10.169
company itself was in a major shock. So the first thing what

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:15.369
I had to do is really to make sure that what happened to the

00:03:15.389 --> 00:03:18.699
organization was never part of the company culture. So the

00:03:18.719 --> 00:03:22.319
first thing what I set out for the organization to clearly

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:28.219
differentiate that we had a major leadership crisis to deal

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:32.659
with and to tackle with, but it was not a company culture

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:37.879
issue per se what we had to deal with. So for me resolving

00:03:37.899 --> 00:03:40.549
this issue in the fastest possible time was obviously the

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:46.239
highest priority. The second one is then when you are the

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:52.599
first one coming from the outside in this way on to the

00:03:52.619 --> 00:03:57.939
management board you know it's the natural thing, I mean who

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:02.779
is person. I didn't have a broad following in Germany

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:06.019
because I spent all my life across the world. So the first

00:04:06.039 --> 00:04:09.319
question was actually who is he. The second question

00:04:09.339 --> 00:04:13.129
probably was, will he bring his own team with him, will he

00:04:13.149 --> 00:04:15.979
change everything what we have done in the past, will he try

00:04:15.979 --> 00:04:20.490
to revolutionize the company or not. So I very clearly set

00:04:20.509 --> 00:04:24.949
out and said Siemens doesn't need a revolution but we will

00:04:24.969 --> 00:04:28.009
continue to have an evolution but we will do this with

00:04:28.029 --> 00:04:36.230
speed, speed, speed, speed. So the third element was then to

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:39.569
tackle the first crisis what I really had to deal with and

00:04:39.589 --> 00:04:42.099
this is this whole corruption case and how to make sure that

00:04:42.099 --> 00:04:47.459
we deal with it in a extremely forceful transparent and open

00:04:47.479 --> 00:04:52.050
manner and I knew that I had to bring in people from the

00:04:52.069 --> 00:04:55.590
outside to help me on this one. So my first key appointments

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:57.939
in the organization were absolute critical and this was

00:04:57.959 --> 00:05:00.319
Peter Solmssen, the Chief Legal Counsel who worked with me

00:05:00.319 --> 00:05:03.990
in GE and then also the Chief Compliance Officer so that I

00:05:04.009 --> 00:05:07.759
had actually a team on to there who I can really trust and

00:05:07.779 --> 00:05:10.410
who can really work with the overall organization to tackle

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:15.920
this most pressing issue what we had to deal with. And then

00:05:15.939 --> 00:05:21.720
you very quickly move on to really say I wanted to have 100

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:25.780
days, I wanted to have 100 days for the simple reason that I

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:32.040
wanted to really see as many people as I could, listen,

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.600
travel around the world, engage the organization from the

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:38.730
customer viewpoint and from the different parts of the world

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.709
get feedback, get input and at the same time work then with

00:05:41.709 --> 00:05:45.319
my top leadership team what has to be changed. And then the

00:05:45.339 --> 00:05:48.850
whole thing evolved around structure changes, leadership

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:52.169
changes and so on and so forth and you have read the case so

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:55.419
I don't have to repeat all of it. But the initial part was

00:05:55.419 --> 00:05:59.990
very much making sure that I am visible to as many people of

00:06:00.009 --> 00:06:03.500
the organization as possible so I had a lot of skip-level

00:06:03.519 --> 00:06:06.680
meetings for example I made deliberate efforts that I made

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:09.569
big town meetings all over the world with being on different

00:06:09.589 --> 00:06:14.300
sites, where I have met the young leaders of the

00:06:14.319 --> 00:06:17.930
organization and so on and so forth that I have really an

00:06:17.949 --> 00:06:20.259
interaction at very different level of the organization and

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:24.339
that I also get an input from the customer viewpoint. So a

00:06:24.359 --> 00:06:27.120
classic day, if you ask me what is a classic day within

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.399
Siemens and I think it's probably still valid today and this

00:06:30.419 --> 00:06:32.949
is what I have basically developed over many, many, many

00:06:32.969 --> 00:06:38.550
years. I come in into the morning, basically start breakfast

00:06:38.569 --> 00:06:42.459
with a group of customers followed by meeting with

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:47.000
politicians or with senior customers on contracts. Then I go

00:06:47.019 --> 00:06:51.560
into having lunch with the young high potentials, me alone

00:06:51.579 --> 00:06:56.040
and nobody else then followed by business reviews, town hall

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:59.550
meetings and then a joint dinner with the top leadership

00:06:59.569 --> 00:07:03.740
team. And if you do this day after day, you get a very, very

00:07:03.759 --> 00:07:06.169
solid and very quick understanding what are the true issues

00:07:06.169 --> 00:07:08.729
and what you have to tackle on and what are the most

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:13.670
pressing ones, what you have to deal with as a leader and as

00:07:13.689 --> 00:07:14.689
a leadership team.

00:07:14.689 --> 00:07:17.689
And as Siemens has evolved over the last three years it has

00:07:17.689 --> 00:07:20.439
400,000 people across the world. How do you affect change

00:07:20.439 --> 00:07:22.819
when you have so many different businesses in so many

00:07:22.819 --> 00:07:25.319
different countries and so many individual people who you

00:07:25.339 --> 00:07:28.560
need to persuade and to get on board with the way you are

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:29.879
thinking about the company?

00:07:29.899 --> 00:07:32.909
I mean the first thing you say is that you have to recognize

00:07:32.909 --> 00:07:36.110
that you have to rely on many, many other people. So you

00:07:36.129 --> 00:07:41.069
need to work one, with a united top leadership team. So the

00:07:41.089 --> 00:07:43.839
first issue what you have to deal with is how do you make

00:07:43.839 --> 00:07:48.730
sure that you really work with your team who you trust and

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:52.629
who speaks with one voice in one direction and acts and

00:07:52.649 --> 00:07:56.490
speaks in the same way. So how do you quickly get basically

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:59.060
different change agents in the organization who are really

00:07:59.079 --> 00:08:03.490
aligned with what you try to accomplish in the organization.

00:08:03.509 --> 00:08:06.259
And for this reason the change that I have introduce in

00:08:06.259 --> 00:08:10.500
terms of the CEO principle was exactly this, to absolutely

00:08:10.519 --> 00:08:14.829
make sure that I had then the leaders in place who I trusted

00:08:14.849 --> 00:08:17.489
who believed in the same vision, who worked in the same

00:08:17.489 --> 00:08:23.579
direction with the same speed how I wanted change happening

00:08:23.599 --> 00:08:26.939
in the organization. And then through the principle that I

00:08:26.959 --> 00:08:30.829
have just announced and I am traveling 70% of my time being

00:08:30.849 --> 00:08:35.519
in front of the organization as much as you can yourself

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:38.539
because there is nothing more important than the personal

00:08:38.559 --> 00:08:43.169
and direct dialogue with the teams around the world.

00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:48.120
And as you have evolved as a leader and if we broaden it out

00:08:48.139 --> 00:08:50.639
just beyond Siemens, could you talk perhaps about how your

00:08:50.639 --> 00:08:53.360
personal style of leadership has evolved overtime and as you

00:08:53.379 --> 00:08:55.189
have encountered different challenges in the different

00:08:55.209 --> 00:08:58.259
companies you have worked in, how has that caused you to

00:08:58.279 --> 00:09:01.070
reflect on your personal style of leadership?

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:04.069
I think it's a very, I mean look at this crowd that I

00:09:04.069 --> 00:09:07.069
always, look at all of you. I mean you are probably, how

00:09:07.069 --> 00:09:11.070
many nations are there in Stanford? 50 nations. So you see

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:15.069
my grandmother is Italian, my parents are Austrian, my wife

00:09:15.089 --> 00:09:17.839
is Spanish, two of my children are American, the third one

00:09:17.839 --> 00:09:21.439
is Spanish and I am Austrian. So I have learned to be a

00:09:21.459 --> 00:09:25.549
minority and I have learned to actually have a very acute

00:09:25.569 --> 00:09:30.350
culture sense and this is very important because the world

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:34.519
is very different. And when you then have the big

00:09:34.539 --> 00:09:37.950
opportunity what I got early on in life to work in Asia, to

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:43.289
study in Asia, in US and in European countries, you are not

00:09:43.309 --> 00:09:50.240
just broadening your skill set, you hone your instinct and

00:09:50.259 --> 00:09:55.539
you also realize that there are many, many avenues to reach

00:09:55.559 --> 00:10:01.629
a certain goal post and you know all of this so I don't have

00:10:01.649 --> 00:10:05.029
to really repeat it. But if I give you three examples, I

00:10:05.049 --> 00:10:09.220
mean in US everybody knows that you can lead very much by

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:12.340
objective setting, vision setting, objective setting and

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:14.949
then you basically work your plan and the people will very

00:10:14.949 --> 00:10:21.620
much align themselves in doing so. And when you then go to

00:10:21.639 --> 00:10:26.899
Spain for example, do we have Spanish colleagues here? So

00:10:26.919 --> 00:10:30.309
the first thing that you realize is that you have to allow

00:10:30.329 --> 00:10:38.990
far more time for talking. So when you tried to align the

00:10:39.009 --> 00:10:42.720
organization, the first principle that you have to adhere to

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:46.500
is that you allow the teams to really have a good talk among

00:10:46.519 --> 00:10:51.600
themselves. And then this is how you create alignment but

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:54.089
it's very, very important that you give this, you cannot

00:10:54.089 --> 00:10:59.080
just set it from the top and say this is what we will do and

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:02.350
this is how it looks like and these are the action steps and

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:05.939
I ask you to follow, because they won't. But you get a very

00:11:05.959 --> 00:11:10.120
strong buy in if you do it the other way around. When you

00:11:10.139 --> 00:11:14.220
then go to Asia, you have a very similar type of leadership

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:18.519
requirement; you must make sure that you engage the

00:11:18.539 --> 00:11:22.559
organization from a bottom up perspective and then align it

00:11:22.579 --> 00:11:28.449
in different initiatives, top down and bottom up. And this

00:11:28.469 --> 00:11:32.819
is the privilege that I had in my life that I grew up in

00:11:32.839 --> 00:11:35.009
different cultural environments, I worked in different

00:11:35.029 --> 00:11:37.759
environments and this helped me a lot in terms of how I

00:11:37.759 --> 00:11:42.689
appreciate the diversity of the world and also how to be

00:11:42.709 --> 00:11:46.730
most effective in cross-cultural teams which is the true

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:49.819
asset of a global company like ours.

00:11:49.839 --> 00:11:53.120
And if we talk about you have such a large organization, you

00:11:53.139 --> 00:11:56.169
talked about the young leaders that you have. I think for a

00:11:56.189 --> 00:11:58.990
group of high potential leaders like us perhaps you could

00:11:59.009 --> 00:12:00.009
talk about-

00:12:00.009 --> 00:12:05.580
But anyway this is exactly what I assume because otherwise

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:08.679
you wouldn't be here.

00:12:08.699 --> 00:12:11.620
But let's talk about the other side of that. What do you

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:14.120
think are the most common mistakes that you see young

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:18.279
leaders in your organization make and what could we learn

00:12:18.299 --> 00:12:21.110
from those mistakes?

00:12:21.129 --> 00:12:24.850
Look I mean the first advice that I would have is I mean all

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:28.230
of you are leaders otherwise you wouldn't be here. But I

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:31.870
think as leaders being young or older the first thing what

00:12:31.889 --> 00:12:38.269
we have to recognize is how privileged we are. And I give

00:12:38.289 --> 00:12:42.600
you now a quote what was a great learning for me from Jack

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:47.049
Welch. I met Jack in the mid 90s through a benchmarking

00:12:47.069 --> 00:12:52.590
exercise. When I then joined the GE organization, he was on

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:57.649
his book tour and I met him in his signing with his latest

00:12:57.669 --> 00:13:01.309
books. So I said Jack I am here now in office of GE, what is

00:13:01.309 --> 00:13:05.289
your one line of advice? You know what he gave me in one

00:13:05.309 --> 00:13:12.569
line of advice? Peter, give it all, Jack. And I think lift

00:13:12.589 --> 00:13:17.370
your dreams and make sure that you live up to the capacity

00:13:17.389 --> 00:13:20.309
and what you have as individuals and make a real

00:13:20.329 --> 00:13:25.379
contribution to the work what you do for others. And I think

00:13:25.399 --> 00:13:28.519
this is the great privilege what we have, this is what

00:13:28.539 --> 00:13:32.120
leaders have to think of in terms of what is their impact

00:13:32.139 --> 00:13:36.429
not just from a economic perspective but also ecological

00:13:36.449 --> 00:13:40.210
perspective, social perspective and the impact what we have

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:43.199
for many, many millions of people. And for me, it was always

00:13:43.199 --> 00:13:46.199
a great privilege. I spent many, many years of my life in

00:13:46.199 --> 00:13:50.949
the pharmaceutical business for a very simple reason because

00:13:50.969 --> 00:13:56.909
I was enormously privileged to work on technologies and help

00:13:56.929 --> 00:14:01.500
people to live a better life and to survive. And when you

00:14:01.519 --> 00:14:04.460
then talk to patients and you feel really the impact what

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:09.850
you have, this makes me very, very proud of. And that's a

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:12.309
great privilege of being the CEO of Siemens being in so many

00:14:12.309 --> 00:14:16.629
businesses, this is the impact which a big organization like

00:14:16.649 --> 00:14:19.419
ours have is enormous and that's an enormous, enormous

00:14:19.439 --> 00:14:20.439
privilege.

00:14:20.439 --> 00:14:24.189
But I guess with that privilege comes a lot of

00:14:24.209 --> 00:14:27.279
responsibility in leaders and when you look at these high

00:14:27.299 --> 00:14:29.549
potential leaders you mentioned you have lunch with them

00:14:29.549 --> 00:14:34.240
many days. I think a lot of us feel that we learn the most

00:14:34.259 --> 00:14:36.929
when we struggle and when we face challenges, perhaps you

00:14:36.949 --> 00:14:39.889
could talk a little bit more about when you talk to these,

00:14:39.889 --> 00:14:42.139
what are the challenges that they are dealing with because

00:14:42.139 --> 00:14:44.959
these I think are the challenges that we might face in the

00:14:44.959 --> 00:14:47.459
next two to three years?

00:14:47.479 --> 00:14:50.709
I always tell my team when I look into this room here how

00:14:50.709 --> 00:14:58.169
many of you will join big global organizations like Siemens.

00:14:58.189 --> 00:15:04.259
One, two, three, four, five, and that's the reality; you

00:15:04.279 --> 00:15:10.029
want to become entrepreneurs, you look for very different

00:15:10.049 --> 00:15:14.079
fulfillments in your life. I mean when I was at Harvard and

00:15:14.099 --> 00:15:17.569
we were talking about when you go through the last 20 years,

00:15:17.589 --> 00:15:22.240
I mean 20 years ago poverty was the issue. At one point

00:15:22.259 --> 00:15:25.399
there were the big organizations then there was the whole

00:15:25.419 --> 00:15:29.129
wave of the consultancies then was a big wave of the

00:15:29.149 --> 00:15:37.199
financial industry and the most promising perspective right

00:15:37.219 --> 00:15:41.909
now is to be real entrepreneurship. So making it for

00:15:41.929 --> 00:15:46.620
yourself and create something for yourself and you are here

00:15:46.639 --> 00:15:49.939
in your backyards at Silicon Valley, and I was quite

00:15:49.959 --> 00:15:52.649
impressed. I learned from the dean actually

00:15:52.669 --> 00:15:55.350
[inaudible] that 40% of you will actually start to work

00:15:55.370 --> 00:16:02.750
there, that's great. So for a big organization like Siemens,

00:16:02.769 --> 00:16:08.539
you are not waiting till you are middle aged and gray till

00:16:08.559 --> 00:16:10.750
somebody in life will tell you and now you are fully

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:15.049
responsible. You are asking for the responsibility far

00:16:15.069 --> 00:16:20.089
earlier than any generation before you. You also have an

00:16:20.109 --> 00:16:24.939
enormous privilege like all of us when we compare the change

00:16:24.959 --> 00:16:27.819
what is now happening in the world be it technological

00:16:27.839 --> 00:16:32.459
change, be it the global change which is happening, it is

00:16:32.479 --> 00:16:38.179
happening in one management generation. The type of change

00:16:38.199 --> 00:16:41.639
which used to happen

00:16:41.659 --> 00:16:44.779
[inaudible] so there is an enormous compressed way of

00:16:44.799 --> 00:16:47.740
opportunity. There is an enormous opportunity space. So big

00:16:47.759 --> 00:16:53.059
organizations have to make sure how they are attractive for

00:16:53.079 --> 00:16:57.149
a young population like yourself who is asking for broader

00:16:57.169 --> 00:17:00.759
experience and more responsibility far earlier than any

00:17:00.779 --> 00:17:04.670
other management generation before you and that's the

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:08.910
challenge and that's the type of opportunity space which big

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:10.990
companies have to face.

00:17:11.009 --> 00:17:14.119
And when we look at this next generation leaders, how do you

00:17:14.139 --> 00:17:16.899
think the challenges that they will face as leaders, you

00:17:16.919 --> 00:17:19.220
have talked about them having more responsibility earlier

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:21.700
but how do you see the other challenges they face as leaders

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:25.160
has being different from the current generation and from

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:27.430
generations before them?

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:32.220
I think as I said I mean the big change is if you just talk

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:34.910
about the globalization which is currently happening in the

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:42.740
world, it's with a speed which is enormous. I grew up for

00:17:42.759 --> 00:17:46.889
example, in my first studies, I studies in Austria and the

00:17:46.909 --> 00:17:50.460
Iron Curtain was actually just happening in the backyards of

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:55.759
Vienna and I was crossing this Iron Curtain several times. I

00:17:55.779 --> 00:17:58.769
could not imagine, really I could not imagine that I will

00:17:58.789 --> 00:18:02.480
ever see in my life that this would change. This for me was

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:06.639
something which was given and look what happened just

00:18:06.659 --> 00:18:10.170
through this one thing how the world has changed. Now we are

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:14.410
going through a major crisis and we see all of a sudden the

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:17.920
political architecture changing dramatically going from a

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:24.279
G7, G8 to a G20, the emerging markets they are asking for

00:18:24.299 --> 00:18:26.299
far more responsibility not just economically but also

00:18:26.299 --> 00:18:31.480
politically. When you then look where 97% of incremental

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:34.639
population growth will happen, it will happen in the

00:18:34.659 --> 00:18:42.409
emerging economies and they want to have the same way and

00:18:42.429 --> 00:18:48.759
opportunity space that we have in our lifespan sitting here

00:18:48.779 --> 00:18:52.619
at the university. So the change that you have and the

00:18:52.639 --> 00:18:56.259
impact, I would not like to talk in terms of challenges but

00:18:56.279 --> 00:19:00.470
the opportunities that you have is the impact what Friedman

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:04.940
was talking about, a flat world which is absolutely a

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:08.299
reality right now. The impact that new technology can bring

00:19:08.319 --> 00:19:15.240
in terms of mass impact is in a way democratized like never

00:19:15.259 --> 00:19:18.279
before and this creates an enormous new opportunity space

00:19:18.299 --> 00:19:23.450
for each one of you and find your space, go for it and make

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:27.279
an impact, and make an impact economically but also socially

00:19:27.299 --> 00:19:30.619
and community wise and I think this is exactly what this

00:19:30.639 --> 00:19:32.430
opportunity space is.

00:19:32.450 --> 00:19:35.980
I think in order to do that all of us have benefited from

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.769
mentors in our life to provide us guidance and to help us

00:19:38.769 --> 00:19:41.720
understand the potential that exists for us. You mentioned

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:44.329
Jack Welch earlier; are there any particular mentors that

00:19:44.349 --> 00:19:47.519
you have had and what do you think were the best important

00:19:47.539 --> 00:19:52.359
things you learned from them?

00:19:52.379 --> 00:19:56.179
I mean first of all I didn't have, I mean I don't go back

00:19:56.179 --> 00:19:58.909
and say this was one mentor which was great. But you learn

00:19:58.909 --> 00:20:02.809
from each one of the people you interface with. Maybe one

00:20:02.829 --> 00:20:08.170
story which was for me very telling; when I was 27 years of

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:11.839
age, I joined a big German company called Hoechst at that

00:20:11.859 --> 00:20:16.779
time, it was a big chemical company and the CEO used to be

00:20:16.799 --> 00:20:20.649
my mentor. He hired me and he said look, start working, work

00:20:20.669 --> 00:20:23.419
here six months and then come back and then let's talk about

00:20:23.419 --> 00:20:27.889
what you would like to do within our organization. I had my

00:20:27.909 --> 00:20:30.889
one page and I was clearing mapping it out what I would like

00:20:30.889 --> 00:20:34.529
to do and this and this and he showed up in his office and

00:20:34.549 --> 00:20:37.889
then he was listening two minutes and then he turned it away

00:20:37.909 --> 00:20:48.509
and said I will give you an advice, have fun, enjoy the

00:20:48.529 --> 00:20:52.950
things that you are doing and make sure that people you work

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:56.730
with are equally enjoyable and then everything that we have

00:20:56.750 --> 00:21:00.619
written here will happen automatically. And that's a great

00:21:00.639 --> 00:21:06.629
advice because never try to plan any career, never try to

00:21:06.649 --> 00:21:10.039
chart but whatever you do try to do it in the best possible

00:21:10.059 --> 00:21:15.259
way and then go for it, it's a great advice that I got for

00:21:15.279 --> 00:21:16.279
myself.

00:21:16.279 --> 00:21:18.670
And as you have become a senior-

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:23.099
And by the way, at that time, my ambition level was to

00:21:23.119 --> 00:21:25.730
become the CEO of Hoechst Austria.

00:21:25.750 --> 00:21:41.289
I guess you are perhaps now a mentor to other people in your

00:21:41.309 --> 00:21:44.180
organization, could you perhaps talk about how you feel

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:46.569
playing that role now as being a mentor to that next

00:21:46.569 --> 00:21:47.569
generation of leaders?

00:21:47.569 --> 00:21:51.359
Yeah this is very important. I mean when you ask me what is

00:21:51.379 --> 00:21:54.440
one of the most rewarding aspects that I spend time with is

00:21:54.460 --> 00:22:00.669
really we have now introduced a new mentoring scheme for all

00:22:00.689 --> 00:22:04.019
the board members, for all my colleagues as well where I

00:22:04.039 --> 00:22:09.379
have basically 15 mentees all across the world with

00:22:09.399 --> 00:22:14.549
different backgrounds and we rotate them every two years. So

00:22:14.569 --> 00:22:17.569
they are mentees for two years and then they are rotated to

00:22:17.569 --> 00:22:21.329
the next, and this is for me extremely rewarding. So I have

00:22:21.349 --> 00:22:27.799
very-very closely and the mentee is responsible for the

00:22:27.819 --> 00:22:31.200
mentor relationship. It works in different ways, it is very

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:34.649
personal and I try to provide feedback to them, give them

00:22:34.669 --> 00:22:38.470
advice from my experience and also at the same time get to

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:42.409
know where their challenges are and to make sure that the

00:22:42.429 --> 00:22:46.990
right steps are taken and that they are stretched in the

00:22:47.009 --> 00:22:49.259
right possible way within the organization. So the mentee-

00:22:49.259 --> 00:22:53.089
mentor relationship is something which is enormously

00:22:53.109 --> 00:22:58.169
enjoyable for me personally and I spend quite significant

00:22:58.189 --> 00:23:01.289
part of my time to make sure that I stay in touch with them

00:23:01.289 --> 00:23:03.119
and that we have a continuous dialogue.

00:23:03.139 --> 00:23:08.000
And before we open it questions, one last thing that we have

00:23:08.019 --> 00:23:11.289
seen in the last five years is a much closer tie between

00:23:11.309 --> 00:23:14.579
business and government. What has been your experience of

00:23:14.599 --> 00:23:17.579
that trend and how do you think that's likely to evolve in

00:23:17.579 --> 00:23:19.079
the next 5 to 10 years?

00:23:19.079 --> 00:23:24.009
Oh absolutely. I mean it's absolutely true number one

00:23:24.029 --> 00:23:26.230
particularly for big infrastructure companies like

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:29.299
ourselves. When you think about a big company like Siemens

00:23:29.319 --> 00:23:32.069
who at the end of the day our whole business is

00:23:32.069 --> 00:23:35.359
infrastructure at the end of the day be it medical

00:23:35.379 --> 00:23:37.629
infrastructure, be it energy infrastructure or be it now

00:23:37.629 --> 00:23:40.190
with Masdar City the next generation of the city environment

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:43.419
so all of it is really big infrastructure programs. And

00:23:43.439 --> 00:23:48.000
there you have on one side you face governments as

00:23:48.019 --> 00:23:52.909
regulators, you face government as customers, the whole

00:23:52.929 --> 00:23:56.750
stimulus programs as part of what we have seen during the

00:23:56.769 --> 00:24:05.059
crisis so absolutely and then you have economists who are

00:24:05.079 --> 00:24:08.819
very closely linked between infrastructure development

00:24:08.839 --> 00:24:12.039
programs and the political agenda around the world. So there

00:24:12.059 --> 00:24:14.720
is a very close alignment, it's getting closer and it's a

00:24:14.720 --> 00:24:16.119
very important one particularly for a company like

00:24:16.119 --> 00:24:17.119
ourselves.

00:24:17.119 --> 00:24:18.119
(Informal Talk)

00:24:18.119 --> 00:24:38.559
[Audience] Thank you very much. I wanted to ask you what

00:24:38.579 --> 00:24:44.059
cleantech opportunities do you see for Latin America.

00:24:44.079 --> 00:24:55.819
I mean I will answer this question but it will start a

00:24:55.839 --> 00:24:58.089
little bit broader because it's a very important question

00:24:58.089 --> 00:25:02.240
that you are asking. When I joined Siemens, I asked a

00:25:02.259 --> 00:25:05.769
question what is our cleantech portfolio and nobody could

00:25:05.789 --> 00:25:10.220
give me an answer what a he cleantech portfolio is because

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:15.359
it was actually in the businesses. There were different

00:25:15.379 --> 00:25:18.319
solutions but nobody actually looked across and said this is

00:25:18.339 --> 00:25:21.579
our cleantech portfolio be it renewable or be it energy

00:25:21.599 --> 00:25:25.639
efficient. So when we have for the first time lined it up so

00:25:25.659 --> 00:25:29.879
I started in 2007 I asked this question. By July 2008 we had

00:25:29.899 --> 00:25:33.869
for the first time actually developed our cleantech

00:25:33.889 --> 00:25:39.339
portfolio and it was 17 billion euro, the biggest and

00:25:39.359 --> 00:25:43.099
broadest cleantech portfolio for any company in the world.

00:25:43.119 --> 00:25:47.419
In the meantime, we have setout an objective of 25 billion,

00:25:47.439 --> 00:25:53.759
we will overachieve it already this year and we are by far

00:25:53.779 --> 00:25:57.349
the biggest infrastructure company and this is what I set

00:25:57.369 --> 00:26:01.909
out as a vision for the company to be a green infrastructure

00:26:01.929 --> 00:26:05.399
pioneer. So that's the major, major strategic emphasis of

00:26:05.419 --> 00:26:08.599
the company across all business lines. To make sure that the

00:26:08.619 --> 00:26:12.710
next generation of product development are more resource

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:16.789
efficient and energy efficient and when we have the highest

00:26:16.809 --> 00:26:20.619
speed trains and the highest speed trains are only consuming

00:26:20.639 --> 00:26:27.019
0.33 liters per 100 kilometers per passenger then it's the

00:26:27.039 --> 00:26:29.809
most energy efficient high speed train as well not just the

00:26:29.829 --> 00:26:35.250
fastest one. And when you look into the sustainability

00:26:35.269 --> 00:26:41.149
agenda around the world, it is blatant clear that this is on

00:26:41.169 --> 00:26:45.700
the highest political priority level despite the fact that

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:48.950
Copenhagen has not succeeded to bring into any climate

00:26:48.970 --> 00:26:53.539
change agreement. But, when you talk about to mayors around

00:26:53.559 --> 00:26:59.289
the world that 1% of the population of square meter, our

00:26:59.309 --> 00:27:03.190
city is consuming 75% of energy consumption, 80% of CO2

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:06.029
emissions, you know that this is the biggest challenge

00:27:06.049 --> 00:27:09.829
around the world. And in south and Latin America and this is

00:27:09.849 --> 00:27:16.109
all around the world. When you then talk about and you talk

00:27:16.129 --> 00:27:23.490
about the Olympic games for example Brazil or the Football

00:27:23.509 --> 00:27:28.750
World Cup, the big resource and the infrastructure

00:27:28.769 --> 00:27:32.230
development programs which are currently put into place have

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:38.029
all the agenda of making sure that the latest generation of

00:27:38.049 --> 00:27:42.859
infrastructure development is significantly better in terms

00:27:42.879 --> 00:27:45.159
of sustainability of the infrastructure development programs

00:27:45.179 --> 00:27:48.559
than any other generation before. So you have this, in Latin

00:27:48.579 --> 00:27:52.490
America equally, for example last week I was together with

00:27:52.509 --> 00:27:58.000
Pinera, the President of Chile, again we immediately worked

00:27:58.019 --> 00:28:03.179
on a program how can actually Chile now introduce the latest

00:28:03.199 --> 00:28:06.579
technology of cleantech to really make sure that they

00:28:06.599 --> 00:28:10.700
benefit for example of their solar intensity that they have

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:14.220
and how can they bring together solar programs, that they

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:18.119
make sure that in their biggest cities are the mobility

00:28:18.139 --> 00:28:20.720
infrastructure programs which are now built out with a

00:28:20.740 --> 00:28:30.099
latest technology of far more resources and consuming less

00:28:30.119 --> 00:28:33.409
resources than before. So it's a big, big program across all

00:28:33.429 --> 00:28:37.839
political scopes be it in Latin America, be it in this

00:28:37.859 --> 00:28:41.919
country. I mean Obama has made a big, big, big push in terms

00:28:41.939 --> 00:28:47.200
of cleantech being not just being a big job engine, how the

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:51.869
economy will be revived; if you go to Asia, to China for

00:28:51.889 --> 00:28:56.069
example, it's a massive government agenda. So you have it

00:28:56.089 --> 00:28:57.789
all over the world and you definitely see it also in Latin

00:28:57.789 --> 00:28:58.789
America.

00:28:58.789 --> 00:29:07.799
[Audience] Hi. Thank you very much for being here with us

00:29:07.819 --> 00:29:10.579
Mr. Loescher. A question I have for you is the following.

00:29:10.599 --> 00:29:15.279
Over the past 100 years, the German precise traditional way

00:29:15.299 --> 00:29:18.909
of doing things has served Siemens very well in developing

00:29:18.929 --> 00:29:22.179
all this infrastructure that we see. Now we see a bigger

00:29:22.199 --> 00:29:26.579
trend towards need for innovation and more high speed. How

00:29:26.599 --> 00:29:31.879
do you manage to change that culture and foster this kind of

00:29:31.899 --> 00:29:35.450
Silicon Valley sort of culture inside such a big company?

00:29:35.470 --> 00:29:41.069
That's a very important question because at the end of the

00:29:41.089 --> 00:29:46.939
day, in any company, 90% of the innovation is happening

00:29:46.959 --> 00:29:51.689
outside the walls of the company. So we have a big program

00:29:51.709 --> 00:29:54.129
for example currently what we call internally open

00:29:54.149 --> 00:29:57.700
innovation historically our laps which we have already all

00:29:57.720 --> 00:30:00.730
over the world, we have one here on the West Coast, we have

00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:03.980
one in Princeton on the East Coast, we have it in Asia, we

00:30:03.980 --> 00:30:06.980
have it in Germany, we have it in 30 countries around the

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:08.980
world where we have research and development activities

00:30:08.980 --> 00:30:12.250
ongoing currently. Number one, the network will be wider

00:30:12.269 --> 00:30:15.569
historically. Our research and developers have very much

00:30:15.589 --> 00:30:20.480
focused their time on what is happening in their own laps

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:24.250
and now the focus is shifting and this is why we call it

00:30:24.269 --> 00:30:27.789
open innovation that we make sure where is in all the

00:30:27.809 --> 00:30:32.250
technology fields which are in our sphere of interest, where

00:30:32.269 --> 00:30:38.689
are the big partner networks, outside partner networks that

00:30:38.709 --> 00:30:42.439
we have to link with to make sure that we innovate faster

00:30:42.459 --> 00:30:46.789
relative to all competitors. So we have a big push basically

00:30:46.809 --> 00:30:51.139
driving the innovation agenda outside our own founder

00:30:51.159 --> 00:30:54.849
engaging with big research institutions or big universities

00:30:54.869 --> 00:30:59.379
or small companies and innovative companies to make sure

00:30:59.399 --> 00:31:04.689
that we have the most innovative network available to us in

00:31:04.709 --> 00:31:09.589
the big technology fields which are of our interest. That's

00:31:09.609 --> 00:31:15.039
a very important push, it's a very strategic one and this

00:31:15.059 --> 00:31:19.619
will be very important because there is nothing more

00:31:19.639 --> 00:31:23.379
democratic than innovation process around the world. And

00:31:23.399 --> 00:31:27.819
when you are looking to where big, big innovative centers

00:31:27.839 --> 00:31:30.789
are happening of course it's United States but when you just

00:31:30.789 --> 00:31:36.939
go by sheer size, if I give you the numbers right now, you

00:31:36.959 --> 00:31:40.990
have just graduations, okay I am talking about technical

00:31:41.009 --> 00:31:44.849
graduations; in Germany, we have roughly 45000 to 50000

00:31:44.869 --> 00:31:51.009
technical graduates every year, in India there are 320,000,

00:31:51.029 --> 00:31:55.949
in China you talk about 800,000, there is a range between

00:31:55.969 --> 00:32:06.029
800,000 and closer to a million. So we see very much the

00:32:06.049 --> 00:32:13.829
gravity centers of innovation changing, shifting and we have

00:32:13.849 --> 00:32:16.730
to take advantage of the fact that we are already present in

00:32:16.730 --> 00:32:19.319
190 countries of the world and therefore we should have a

00:32:19.319 --> 00:32:22.619
very good advantage with great reputation in these markets

00:32:22.639 --> 00:32:32.279
that we are able to tap into these new networks. And then we

00:32:32.299 --> 00:32:35.939
have also a venture capital fund internally where for

00:32:35.959 --> 00:32:40.980
example the fund is available for internal research programs

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:43.859
as well as for partner companies as well.

00:32:43.879 --> 00:32:50.279
[Audience] Hello sir, thank you. You talk here about, at the

00:32:50.299 --> 00:32:53.470
beginning you said setting vision and having clarity and

00:32:53.490 --> 00:32:56.759
strong leadership and I think very charismatic leadership

00:32:56.779 --> 00:33:00.329
but in some of our classes especially in OB we learn that

00:33:00.349 --> 00:33:06.750
sometimes it's good to use the high skills of the group to

00:33:06.769 --> 00:33:10.230
be facilitative, to be participative. So how do you balance

00:33:10.250 --> 00:33:11.250
those two?

00:33:11.250 --> 00:33:18.909
Yeah but this is exactly what I mean. I think at the end of

00:33:18.929 --> 00:33:23.240
the day it's always the team, okay. It doesn't matter who it

00:33:23.259 --> 00:33:28.619
is, an individual never really matters as much as any high

00:33:28.639 --> 00:33:31.819
performance team and this is why I have also pushed for

00:33:31.839 --> 00:33:35.619
example in the organization for diversity. I am on record as

00:33:35.639 --> 00:33:41.029
I said the management is too white, too male, too German

00:33:41.049 --> 00:33:43.799
when I joined the organization because I think you have to

00:33:43.809 --> 00:33:46.649
tap into the diversity pool. What you have is a global

00:33:46.669 --> 00:33:50.049
organization and when you have the great privilege that you

00:33:50.069 --> 00:33:53.319
are at home in 190 countries of the world, that you are able

00:33:53.329 --> 00:33:56.220
to track the best talents, to develop the best talents and

00:33:56.240 --> 00:34:01.389
make them available for the global organization from a team

00:34:01.409 --> 00:34:05.679
perspective as well as from an individual perspective. This

00:34:05.699 --> 00:34:10.989
is a very-very critical element and we have on top of

00:34:11.009 --> 00:34:13.699
individual responsibilities what you would be responsible

00:34:13.719 --> 00:34:16.710
for, many-many programs where we particularly look into

00:34:16.730 --> 00:34:20.400
initiatives, corporate leadership and pulling into these

00:34:20.420 --> 00:34:25.009
initiatives very diverse teams from all over the world to

00:34:25.029 --> 00:34:29.039
really help us to drive the change agenda. So that's at the

00:34:29.059 --> 00:34:33.549
cornerstone of what we try to do. But this is always in the

00:34:33.569 --> 00:34:37.579
framework of the clarity of vision what you try to have of

00:34:37.599 --> 00:34:41.929
an organization. So the teams, they really have to work

00:34:41.949 --> 00:34:46.639
towards the ultimate aim of the focus of the overall

00:34:46.659 --> 00:34:51.170
organization. But leveraging the diversity of team, I mean I

00:34:51.190 --> 00:34:53.860
don't have to explain this, this is probably one of the

00:34:53.860 --> 00:34:56.159
great-great privilege that you have of working together.

00:34:56.179 --> 00:35:02.889
[Audience] Thank you sir. My question is looking forward to

00:35:02.909 --> 00:35:06.799
the next 10 years what is the single most important

00:35:06.819 --> 00:35:10.849
strategic issue that you have to face as a CEO and how are

00:35:10.869 --> 00:35:17.590
you gearing the company to actually address that issue?

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:26.980
It's the wrong question, I will tell you why. Because as an

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:30.230
innovation company which is an infrastructure company, the

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:34.259
innovation cycles of many of our products are going for

00:35:34.279 --> 00:35:38.250
example for 10 years, 15 years, 20 years. For example, we

00:35:38.269 --> 00:35:41.299
are now bringing to the market the most energy efficient gas

00:35:41.319 --> 00:35:46.110
turbine of the world. The initial part of the development

00:35:46.130 --> 00:35:50.039
programs goes back to the early 90s. When we talk about the

00:35:50.059 --> 00:35:54.409
current high speed train which we are currently selling, the

00:35:54.429 --> 00:36:00.159
initial development programs go back to the mid '90s. So

00:36:00.179 --> 00:36:05.150
when we talk about the innovation agenda to be innovation

00:36:05.170 --> 00:36:09.960
leaders in the fields where we have focused on in the three

00:36:09.980 --> 00:36:16.480
sectors, we are looking way above the 10 years time horizon,

00:36:16.500 --> 00:36:22.480
we really have to look into 20 years. You can look from how

00:36:22.500 --> 00:36:26.380
you implement in terms of this the different strategies, you

00:36:26.400 --> 00:36:29.440
can look 5 to 10 years but when you then say okay what is

00:36:29.440 --> 00:36:32.639
our innovation advantage that we would like to see in this

00:36:32.659 --> 00:36:38.059
area, the time horizon is way, way longer than 10 years. So

00:36:38.079 --> 00:36:44.190
we set out as the strategic direction for the company being

00:36:44.210 --> 00:36:49.170
the green infrastructure pioneer. So the pioneering spirit

00:36:49.190 --> 00:36:53.960
what you have read in the case is very much at the forefront

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:59.170
what we try to drive. So there is a big innovation agenda in

00:36:59.190 --> 00:37:03.769
all areas. There is not a single business field that we are

00:37:03.789 --> 00:37:07.679
looking for but we are looking to really making sure that

00:37:07.699 --> 00:37:12.329
the energy efficiency of all products going forward are

00:37:12.349 --> 00:37:16.569
significantly more energy efficient and that we drive the

00:37:16.589 --> 00:37:20.619
organization towards tailor-made solutions for big, big

00:37:20.639 --> 00:37:27.279
customers for example, which are not in the forefront of a

00:37:27.299 --> 00:37:31.920
classic Siemens customer. A classical Siemens customer would

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:35.230
be if there is tender, if there is Palo Alto or anywhere

00:37:35.250 --> 00:37:38.529
else and they would say there is a great train that we would

00:37:38.549 --> 00:37:41.549
now like to develop and there is a tender process and then

00:37:41.549 --> 00:37:46.460
we would be off and running going after the tender. But to

00:37:46.480 --> 00:37:53.699
engage Masdar City and to say what can we drive together to

00:37:53.719 --> 00:37:57.949
help you to implement division of a CO2 free city

00:37:57.969 --> 00:38:02.730
environment and what can we from a technology perspective

00:38:02.750 --> 00:38:06.880
implement to link together the energy smart grid

00:38:06.900 --> 00:38:11.110
infrastructure with the smart building infrastructure that

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:15.119
all the energy consumption which is happening in the city is

00:38:15.139 --> 00:38:20.519
able to communicate with each other and being optimized in

00:38:20.539 --> 00:38:26.299
terms of the energy efficiency of buildings, of energy flows

00:38:26.319 --> 00:38:30.170
across the city and therefore ultimately helps to drive

00:38:30.190 --> 00:38:35.829
massively down the energy costs of Masdar City. This is an

00:38:35.849 --> 00:38:38.500
innovation partnership what we are now pulling together with

00:38:38.519 --> 00:38:41.690
Masdar City. Classically speaking, there is no tender in

00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:45.259
this context but as I said a big, big strategic alliance

00:38:45.279 --> 00:38:48.589
where we work together with Masdar City to really drive it

00:38:48.609 --> 00:38:51.900
and that's at the forefront what Siemens is all about or

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:58.940
Desertec where we basically try to harness the sun of Africa

00:38:58.960 --> 00:39:02.339
not just for the African countries themselves but also

00:39:02.359 --> 00:39:07.069
ultimately being available for importing towards Europe is a

00:39:07.089 --> 00:39:11.969
big vision which is 10 to 15 years out where we work with an

00:39:11.989 --> 00:39:14.539
innovation partnership together with the governments of

00:39:14.559 --> 00:39:18.309
Africa and with the European Union what is the right funding

00:39:18.329 --> 00:39:21.750
which is required, what is the technology which is already

00:39:21.769 --> 00:39:24.949
today available, High Voltage Direct Current technology so

00:39:24.969 --> 00:39:29.670
that we can transmit energy without any losses over long

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:33.989
periods of time. So the big, big programs which are

00:39:34.009 --> 00:39:38.099
infrastructure programs going beyond one customer much more

00:39:38.119 --> 00:39:41.429
into a strategic alliance and innovation partnership is the

00:39:41.449 --> 00:39:44.400
big, big lever for the organization going forward and this

00:39:44.420 --> 00:39:48.750
is where we drive the strategic impact of Siemens versus

00:39:48.769 --> 00:39:52.559
just selling products and services and being attentive to

00:39:52.579 --> 00:39:55.329
tenders which we ultimately obviously have to do in all our

00:39:55.329 --> 00:39:56.329
business lines.

00:39:56.329 --> 00:40:00.769
[Audience] Sir, a quick follow up question. I see that

00:40:00.789 --> 00:40:05.529
vision, can you talk to us just quickly about how you

00:40:05.549 --> 00:40:08.049
translate that vision into execution by breaking it down for

00:40:08.049 --> 00:40:09.860
those--?

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:19.119
So the vision Masdar City was a vision of the Abu Dhabi

00:40:19.139 --> 00:40:26.230
Government. So I engaged very much at the top level of the

00:40:26.250 --> 00:40:31.130
political environment of Abu Dhabi to say let's pull

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:35.960
together a small team of your people who are responsible for

00:40:35.980 --> 00:40:40.489
Masdar City under the leadership Dr. Sultan who is the CEO

00:40:40.509 --> 00:40:43.929
of Masdar City and our team. So, the two of us then pulled

00:40:43.949 --> 00:40:46.829
an operational team together of creating a strategic

00:40:46.849 --> 00:40:50.759
alignment. We worked for 18 months because you have to

00:40:50.779 --> 00:40:54.039
really break it down what are the requirements of the city,

00:40:54.059 --> 00:40:57.059
how is the layout of the city, what are the different steps

00:40:57.059 --> 00:40:59.940
of phase one and phase two, it's now slightly delayed

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:02.750
because of the impact of the current crisis but they are

00:41:02.769 --> 00:41:06.559
continuing with the vision. So we worked for 18 months and a

00:41:06.579 --> 00:41:10.559
week ago I was able to sign it in London. This is how we

00:41:10.579 --> 00:41:13.529
have worked and broken it down within a very specific

00:41:13.549 --> 00:41:21.559
initiative ultimately being able to be signed.

00:41:21.579 --> 00:41:24.000
[Audience] You said earlier one of your five values of

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:29.059
leadership was to have a true north that transcends company

00:41:29.079 --> 00:41:32.619
values, company norms. Could you talk to us about your true

00:41:32.639 --> 00:41:37.539
north how you came to that true north and why that is your

00:41:37.559 --> 00:41:40.769
true north?

00:41:40.789 --> 00:41:46.349
I think this comes back, I say true north because it goes

00:41:46.369 --> 00:41:52.389
back, the first environment where you really learn about it

00:41:52.409 --> 00:41:58.799
are the values within your family. And the first role model

00:41:58.819 --> 00:42:04.339
for me personally was my own father. And then you have a lot

00:42:04.359 --> 00:42:08.059
of opportunities and privileges for a university like

00:42:08.079 --> 00:42:10.519
Stanford University where you probably have lot of

00:42:10.539 --> 00:42:15.139
leaderships in terms of ethics, ethical behavior and

00:42:15.159 --> 00:42:19.259
business ethics. And I for this reason for example have

00:42:19.279 --> 00:42:24.670
actually sponsored also an educational initiative at the

00:42:24.690 --> 00:42:30.159
Technical University of Munich to really provide these

00:42:30.179 --> 00:42:34.759
aspects at the university level because at the end of the

00:42:34.779 --> 00:42:38.989
day this goes back to families, the values of the families

00:42:39.009 --> 00:42:41.630
and the values of the education that you were brought up

00:42:41.630 --> 00:42:46.789
with. And then many of you I assure you, many of you in your

00:42:46.809 --> 00:42:52.819
life will be faced in one way or another to make a personal

00:42:52.839 --> 00:43:01.849
decision do I do something or I don't in different contexts

00:43:01.869 --> 00:43:06.929
you know sometimes cultural norms and then you have to say

00:43:06.949 --> 00:43:11.860
no, I live up to my own standards with my own norm and I am

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:14.630
not willing to go for it. For example, this whole bribery

00:43:14.630 --> 00:43:19.089
case in Siemens; when you read the testimonies of people

00:43:19.109 --> 00:43:22.359
this is why I say this was a leadership crisis, this was not

00:43:22.359 --> 00:43:24.859
the cultural crisis of the community but this was a

00:43:24.859 --> 00:43:27.359
leadership attitude of some leaders who have decided a red

00:43:27.359 --> 00:43:30.509
line for me is not a red line. And when you then look into

00:43:30.509 --> 00:43:34.480
the testimonies that they have given, the classical

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:37.559
testimony is well I have done it to the benefit of the

00:43:37.579 --> 00:43:47.670
company. Come on. People were pursuing this because they had

00:43:47.690 --> 00:43:51.809
sales incentives as well and ultimately they had made the

00:43:51.829 --> 00:43:58.880
incentive schemes. So this is what I mean, you don't have to

00:43:58.900 --> 00:44:01.589
talk about company values, this is the next level that you

00:44:01.589 --> 00:44:04.150
then have obviously and I would very much encourage you

00:44:04.170 --> 00:44:08.569
whatever organization you join make sure how are the company

00:44:08.589 --> 00:44:13.049
values not as values but how are they actually lived, who

00:44:13.069 --> 00:44:16.429
are the role models of the organization and who are the role

00:44:16.449 --> 00:44:22.589
models of leaders and can you relate to them. And if

00:44:22.609 --> 00:44:26.679
somebody asks you the most dangerous thing that you will

00:44:26.699 --> 00:44:35.210
encounter is I have to do things because everybody does, and

00:44:35.230 --> 00:44:39.079
it's up to each one of you to decide to say no I don't do

00:44:39.079 --> 00:44:45.009
it. And this is what I mean by true north, I have also

00:44:45.029 --> 00:44:57.529
encountered this in my professional life for sure.

00:44:57.549 --> 00:45:03.389
[Audience] Sir, you spoke about how young people are asking

00:45:03.409 --> 00:45:07.769
for more responsibility at an earlier age. For that model to

00:45:07.789 --> 00:45:12.339
work that means young people have to be able to accept and

00:45:12.359 --> 00:45:15.849
succeed with this more responsibility. So I wonder from your

00:45:15.869 --> 00:45:20.519
point of view do you believe that the skills and abilities

00:45:20.539 --> 00:45:24.739
of young people are changing commensurately with their

00:45:24.759 --> 00:45:28.799
changes and expectations and desires?

00:45:28.819 --> 00:45:35.519
What do you think about you here?

00:45:35.539 --> 00:45:41.349
[Audience] I think it comes back to your point about feeling

00:45:41.369 --> 00:45:45.750
privileged and I think sometimes it's important to recognize

00:45:45.769 --> 00:45:52.210
when we are feeling privileged and so sometimes this leads

00:45:52.230 --> 00:45:58.509
to an expectation that we deserve more. But I also feel and

00:45:58.529 --> 00:46:02.210
I am biased that we are capable of doing great things. So I

00:46:02.230 --> 00:46:05.690
wonder from an executive's point of view how you look at

00:46:05.710 --> 00:46:09.889
young talent that come into your company and hunger for the

00:46:09.909 --> 00:46:13.039
next step that are two steps ahead of them and are they

00:46:13.059 --> 00:46:17.889
ready for that?

00:46:17.909 --> 00:46:21.190
I watch very closely one thing, I watch very closely in

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:28.599
terms of how people are performing in their current jobs and

00:46:28.619 --> 00:46:33.880
my alarm bells are going on if people continuously talk

00:46:33.900 --> 00:46:40.319
about the next step. In some instances it's probably very

00:46:40.339 --> 00:46:44.420
right and then the question is, is the person in a job who

00:46:44.440 --> 00:46:51.429
is too small for him or is he able to actually stretch

00:46:51.449 --> 00:46:58.210
himself or herself at the higher level. That is very

00:46:58.230 --> 00:47:04.940
dangerous. If you try to do things where you do things with

00:47:04.960 --> 00:47:12.980
the anticipation, I have a right of this and that and that,

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:16.639
and this is a very, very valuable question what you ask, a

00:47:16.659 --> 00:47:20.759
very important one and to find the right balance can only be

00:47:20.779 --> 00:47:25.639
given at an individual basis. But never look out doing

00:47:25.659 --> 00:47:30.759
things in a certain way today because I expect something

00:47:30.779 --> 00:47:35.449
from the organization tomorrow. I think this can never work

00:47:35.469 --> 00:47:42.199
and will never work. Rather, try to look into finding an

00:47:42.219 --> 00:47:45.549
environment where you have a mentorship and a leadership

00:47:45.569 --> 00:47:49.420
environment where you can engage yourself and leverage

00:47:49.440 --> 00:47:52.739
yourself early on as part of a great performing team.

00:47:52.759 --> 00:47:58.039
Because early on in life the biggest lever that you have is

00:47:58.059 --> 00:48:00.880
the high performance team aspect which was asked earlier

00:48:00.900 --> 00:48:04.609
here. It's not individual, it's actually the team

00:48:04.629 --> 00:48:11.710
environment and then you will drive on it. The question can

00:48:11.730 --> 00:48:14.170
only be asked individually but it's a very, very important

00:48:14.170 --> 00:48:42.440
question that you have asked.

00:48:42.460 --> 00:48:43.460
[Audience, inaudible]

00:48:43.460 --> 00:48:51.730
The Stuxnet is a great business opportunity because now many

00:48:51.750 --> 00:48:54.509
industrial companies have realized the danger of being

00:48:54.529 --> 00:48:59.099
actually part of a portable computer or of a computer

00:48:59.119 --> 00:49:02.219
environment. And we produce in our automation control

00:49:02.239 --> 00:49:09.569
division actually proprietary solutions and since Stuxnet

00:49:09.589 --> 00:49:14.210
has hit, the drive to the little bit more expensive

00:49:14.230 --> 00:49:20.159
proprietary solutions from Siemens, the demand is going up.

00:49:20.179 --> 00:49:23.369
But it's a serious danger. I mean you have now asked about

00:49:23.369 --> 00:49:29.509
the danger, absolutely it's a danger there is no question.

00:49:29.529 --> 00:49:32.609
And we have now more and more customers who are looking into

00:49:32.629 --> 00:49:36.109
their industrial control environments to really make sure

00:49:36.129 --> 00:49:39.529
where are weak points so we are doing a lot of so-called

00:49:39.549 --> 00:49:43.339
health checks for many of our customers at this moment in

00:49:43.359 --> 00:49:47.900
time because this has obviously created a much heightened

00:49:47.920 --> 00:49:51.440
awareness compared to before.

00:49:51.460 --> 00:49:54.079
I would like to say thank you very much for attending

00:49:54.079 --> 00:49:56.079
[inaudible] today, we really appreciate you doing that.

